  "Attributes": [
    {
      "Id": 759906,
      "attributeType": "LongString",
      "attributeName": "AIDescription",
      "attributeRelationType": "None",
      "Value": "\"168 in 1\" was a hugely popular cartridge for the MT-777DX (a popular Chinese clone of the Nintendo Entertainment System) and other consoles in the early 1990s. More than just a collection, it was a cultural phenomenon, often bundled with consoles and widely available in electronics stores and markets. The cartridge itself was robust, featuring a thick, durable casing and a densely packed circuit board. \n\nThis compilation boasted a massive library of games, aiming to offer incredible value by providing numerous titles for the price of one. While the games were often unlicensed and varied in quality, the sheer volume and the inclusion of highly sought-after titles like *Contra* (prominently featured on the cartridge’s label and in its startup sequence) made it a must-have.  A rear label meticulously listed all included games, a significant benefit for potential buyers. Later iterations of the cartridge experimented with different colors and designs, and eventually, counterfeit versions appeared. The cartridge's appeal lay in its promise of endless gaming possibilities, a nostalgic touchstone for many who experienced the early days of home consoles."
    },
